## Introduction

## Definitions

## 1.1 In this Protocol—

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (1)‘admission of liability’ means the defendant admits that—

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (a)the claimant contracted a gastric illness (as defined below);

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (b)the defendant thereby caused some loss to the claimant, the nature and extent of which is not admitted; and

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (c)the defendant has no accrued defence to the claim under the Limitation Act 1980;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (2)‘bank holiday’ means a bank holiday under the Banking and Financial Dealings Act 1971;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (3)‘business day’ means any day except Saturday, Sunday, a bank holiday, Good Friday or Christmas Day;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (4)‘certificate of recoverable benefits’ has the same meaning as in rule 36.20(1)(e)(i) of the Civil Procedure Rules 1998. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (5)‘child’ means a person under 18;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (6)‘claim’ means a claim, prior to the start of proceedings, for payment of damages under the process set out in this Protocol;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (7)‘claimant’ means a person starting a claim under this Protocol; unless the context indicated that it means the claimant’s legal representative;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (8)‘defendant’ includes, where the context indicates, the defendant’s insurer or legal representative;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (9)‘gastric illness’ means any gastrointestinal illness arising from a breach of contract or breach of statutory duty or common law duty in respect of services, food and beverages provided in relation to a package holiday;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (10)‘legal representative’ has the same meaning as in rule 2.3(1) of the Civil Procedure Rules 1998;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (11)‘medical expert’ means a person who is registered with the General Medical Council;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (12)‘package holiday’ means a package which is regulated by the Package Travel, Package Holidays and Package Tours Regulations 1992 (‘the Package Travel Regulations’) or any subordinate or amending legislation arising from EU Directive 2015/2302;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (13)‘package travel claim’-

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (a)means a claim for damages for gastric illness contracted during a package holiday, and

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (b)may include a claim for diminution in value or loss of enjoyment suffered by the same claimant, but

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (c)excludes a claim under the Athens convention or the Montréal Convention;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (14)‘pecuniary losses’ means past and future expenses and losses;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] (15)‘vulnerable adult’ has the same meaning as in paragraph 3(5) of Schedule 1 to the Legal Aid Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 1.1 In this Protocol—] 1.2 A reference to a rule or practice direction, unless otherwise defined, is a reference to a rule in the Civil Procedure Rules 1998 (‘CPR’) or a practice direction supplementing them. ## Preamble

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Preamble] 2.1 This Protocol describes the behaviour the court expects of the parties prior to the start of proceedings where a claimant claims damages valued at no more than £25,000 in a Package Travel claim. If, at any stage, the claimant values the claim at more than the upper limit of the fast track, the claimant should notify the defendant as soon as possible. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Preamble] 2.2 The Civil Procedure Rules enable the court to impose costs sanctions where either party fails to comply with this Protocol. ## Aims

## 3.1 The aims of this Protocol are to—

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 3.1 The aims of this Protocol are to—] (1)encourage the exchange of early and full information about the claim;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 3.1 The aims of this Protocol are to—] (2)encourage better and earlier pre-action investigation by all parties;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 3.1 The aims of this Protocol are to—] (3)  enable the parties to avoid litigation by agreeing settlement of the dispute before proceedings are commenced;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 3.1 The aims of this Protocol are to—] (4)enable the parties to narrow the issues in dispute before proceedings are commenced; and

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 3.1 The aims of this Protocol are to—] (5)support the proportionate and efficient management of proceedings where litigation cannot be avoided. ## Scope

## 4. 1This protocol applies where—

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 4. 1This protocol applies where—] (1)the claim arises from a gastric illness contracted during a package holiday;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 4. 1This protocol applies where—] (2)no letter of claim has been sent to the defendant before 7th May 2018;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 4. 1This protocol applies where—] (3)the claim includes damages in respect of personal injury;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 4. 1This protocol applies where—] (4)the claimant values the claim at not more than £25,000 on a full liability basis including pecuniary loss but excluding interest (‘the upper limit’), and

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 4. 1This protocol applies where—] (5) if proceedings were started the small claims track would not be the normal track for that claim. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 4. 1This protocol applies where—] (Rule 26.9 provides that the small claims track is not the normal track where the value of any claim for damages for personal injuries (defined as compensation for pain, suffering and loss of amenity), other than a claim arising from a road traffic accident, is more than £1,500.)

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 4. 1This protocol applies where—] 4.2 This Protocol ceases to apply to a claim where, at any stage, the claimant notifies the defendant the claim has been revalued at more than the upper limit. However, the spirit, if not the letter of this Protocol should still be followed for claims which could potentially be allocated to the multi-track or the intermediate track (or small claims track). ## 4.3 This Protocol does not apply to a claim—

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 4.3 This Protocol does not apply to a claim—] (1)where the claimant or defendant acts as personal representative of a deceased person;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 4.3 This Protocol does not apply to a claim—] (2)where the claimant or defendant is a protected party as defined in rule 21.1(2);

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 4.3 This Protocol does not apply to a claim—] (3)where the claimant is bankrupt; or

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 4.3 This Protocol does not apply to a claim—] (4)where the defendant is insolvent and there is no identifiable insurer. ## General provisions

## Time periods

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Time periods] 5.1 Reference to a fixed number of days is a reference to business days as defined in paragraph 1.1(3). [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Time periods] 5.2 Where a party should respond within a fixed number of days, the period for response starts the first business day after the information was sent to that party. ## 5.3 All time periods specified in this Protocol may be varied by agreement between the parties. ## Claimants without a legal representative

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Claimants without a legal representative] 6.1 If a party to a claim does not seek professional advice from a legal representative they should still, in so far as is reasonably possible, comply with the terms of this Protocol. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Claimants without a legal representative] 6.2 If a party to a claim becomes aware that another party is a litigant in person, they should send a copy of this Protocol to the litigant in person at the earliest opportunity. ## The Protocol

## Medical records

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Medical records] 7.1 At the earliest opportunity, legible copies of the claimant’s medical and other records should be placed in an indexed and paginated bundle by the claimant. This bundle should be kept up to date. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Medical records] 7.2 If either the claimant or the defendant considers additional health records are required from a third party, in the first instance these should be requested by or through the claimant. Third party healthcare providers are expected to co-operate. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Medical records] (Rule 31.17 deals with the procedure for applying to the court for disclosure by third parties.)

## Letter of notification

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of notification] 8.1 The claimant or his legal representative may wish to notify a defendant and/or the insurer as soon as they know a claim is likely to be made, but before they are able to send a detailed letter of claim, particularly, for instance, when the defendant has no or limited knowledge of the incident giving rise to the claim, or where the claimant is incurring significant expenditure as a result of the gastric illness which he hopes the defendant might pay for, in whole or in part. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of notification] 8.2 The letter of notification should advise the defendant and/or the insurer of any relevant information that is available to assist with determining issues of liability and the likely size and heads of the claim and the suitability of the claim for an interim payment. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of notification] 8.3 Notification of any related claims for damages including any claims for diminution in value or loss of enjoyment suffered by a party who is not also claiming damages for gastric illness should be sent to the defendant at the same time as the claimant sends notification of the package travel claim. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of notification] 8.4 If the claimant or his legal representative gives notification before sending a letter of claim, it will not start the timetable for the letter of response. However, the letter of notification should be acknowledged by the defendant and/or the defendant’s insurer within 14 days of receipt. ## 8.5 The letter of notification must include the booking number. ## Letter of claim

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of claim] 9.1 The claimant should send to the proposed defendant a letter of claim which must include a clear summary of the facts on which the claim is based including details of the following—

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of claim] (1)the location and date of the alleged breach of duty;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of claim] (2)the nature of the gastric illness suffered, and the way in which this has impacted on the claimant’s day to day functioning and prognosis;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of claim] (3)the date of the onset of symptoms and whether the medical treatment was sought and, if so, when and from whom;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of claim] (4)all attendances on the claimant’s GP and/or hospital attendances and any relevant treatment received with regard to the gastric illness;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of claim] (5)a schedule of any financial loss incurred by the claimant;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of claim] (6)an indication of the further heads of damage to be claimed and the amount of that loss, unless this is impracticable; and

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of claim] (7)any other package travel claims made by the claimant. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of claim] 9.2 Unless the defendant has provided the claimant with an email address to which the letter of claim may be sent, the letter of claim should be sent by first-class post. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of claim] 9.3 A template for the suggested content of the letter of claim is attached at Annex A1. The level of detail may need to be varied to suit the particular circumstances of the case. In all cases there should be sufficient information to enable the defendant to estimate the likely size and heads of the claim without necessarily addressing quantum in detail. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of claim] 9.4 Details of the claimant’s National Insurance number and date of birth should be supplied to the defendant’s insurer once the defendant has responded to the letter of claim and confirmed the identity of the insurer. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of claim] 9.5 Once the claimant has sent the letter of claim no further investigation on liability should normally be carried out within the Protocol period until a response is received from the defendant indicating whether liability is disputed. ## Letter of response

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of response] 10.1 The defendant must acknowledge receipt of the Letter of claim within 42 days of posting of the letter. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of response] 10.2 A template for the suggested content of the letter of response is attached at Annex A2. The level of detail may need to be varied to suit the particular circumstances of the case. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of response] 10.3 The letter of response must confirm if the defendant will be handling the claim or provide particulars of the legal or other representative appointed to act on behalf of the defendant. If the defendant is aware of any significant omissions from the letter of claim they should identify them specifically. Similarly, if they are aware that another defendant has also been identified whom they believe would not be a correct defendant in any proceedings, they should notify the claimant without delay, with reasons, and in any event by the end of the response period. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of response] 10.4 Where there has been no reply by or on behalf of the defendant within 42 days, the claimant will be entitled to issue proceedings. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of response] 10.5 The defendant will have a maximum of six months from the date of acknowledgment of the letter of claim to investigate. No later than the end of that period, the defendant should reply, stating if liability is admitted by admitting that the gastric illness occurred, that it was caused by the defendant’s breach of duty, and the claimant suffered loss and there is no defence under the Limitation Act 1980. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of response] 10.6 If a defendant denies liability and/or causation, their version of events should be supplied. The defendant should also enclose with the response, documents in their possession which are material to the issues between the parties, and which would be likely to be ordered to be disclosed by the court, either on an application for pre-action disclosure, or on disclosure during proceedings. No charge will be made for providing copy documents under the Protocol. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of response] 10.7 An admission made by any party under this Protocol may well be binding on that party in the litigation. Further information about admissions made under this Protocol is to be found in rule 14.1A. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Letter of response] 10.8 Following receipt of the letter of response, if the claimant is aware that there may be a delay of six months or more before the claimant decides if, when and how to proceed, the claimant should keep the defendant generally informed. ## Disclosure

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Disclosure] 11.1 The aim of early disclosure of documents by the defendant is not to encourage ‘fishing expeditions’ by the claimant, but to promote an early exchange of relevant information to help in clarifying or resolving issues in dispute. The claimant’s solicitor can assist by identifying in the letter of claim or in a subsequent letter the particular categories of documents which they consider are relevant and why, with a brief explanation of their purported relevance if necessary. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Disclosure] 11.2 Pre-action disclosure will generally be limited to the documents required to be enclosed with the letter of claim and the Response. In cases where liability and causation are admitted in full, disclosure will be limited to the documents relevant to quantum, but the parties can agree that further disclosure may be given. If either or both of the parties consider that further disclosure should be given but there is disagreement about some aspect of that process, they may be able to make an application to the court for pre-action disclosure under Part 31 of the CPR. Parties should assist each other and avoid the necessity for such an application. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Disclosure] 11.3 Attached at Annex B is a specimen, but non-exhaustive, list of documents likely to be material in a package travel claim. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Disclosure] 11.4 The parties are under a duty to preserve documents that they may be required to disclose. Destruction of such documents may amount to an abuse of the court process. ## Experts

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Experts] 12.1 This Protocol encourages joint selection of, and access to, quantum experts, and, where possible, liability experts. The expert report produced is not a joint report for the purposes of CPR Part 35. The Protocol promotes the practice of the claimant obtaining a medical report, disclosing it to the defendant who then asks questions of the expert and/or agrees it and does not obtain their own report. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Experts] 12.2 Where the duration of the symptoms of gastric illness was not longer than 28 days, it is expected that the medical report will be obtained from a GP. Where symptoms subsist for more than 28 days a report from a GP may be insufficient and the parties are expected to consult and to agree the appropriate speciality of the expert to be instructed to prepare a report. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Experts] 12.3 In most cases, a report from a non-medical expert will not be required, but a report may be obtained where it is reasonably required to determine liability or to value the claim. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Experts] 12.4 Before any party instructs an expert, they should give the other party a list of the name(s) of one or more experts in the relevant speciality whom they consider are suitable to instruct. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Experts] 12.5 Some solicitors choose to obtain medical reports through medical agencies, rather than directly from a specific doctor or hospital. The defendant’s prior consent to this should be sought and, if the defendant so requests, the agency should be asked to provide in advance the names of the doctor(s) whom they are considering instructing. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Experts] 12.6 Within 14 days of providing a list of experts the other party may indicate an objection to one or more of the named experts. The first party should then instruct a mutually acceptable expert assuming there is one (this is not the same as a joint expert). It must be emphasised that when the claimant nominates an expert in the original letter of claim, the defendant has a further 14 days to object to one or more of the named experts after expiration of the 42 day period within which they have to reply to the letter of claim. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Experts] 12.7 If the defendant objects to all the listed experts, the parties may then instruct experts of their own choice. It will be for the court to decide, subsequently and if proceedings are issued, whether either party had acted unreasonably. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Experts] 12.8 If the defendant does not object to an expert nominated by the claimant, they shall not be entitled to rely on their own expert evidence within that expert’s area of expertise unless—

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Experts] (1)the claimant agrees;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Experts] (2)the court so directs; or

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Experts] (3)the claimant’s expert report has been amended and the claimant is not prepared to disclose the original report. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Experts] 12.9 Any party may send to an expert written questions on the report. Such questions must be put within 28 days of service of the expert’s report and must only be for the purpose of clarification of the report. The expert should send answers to the questions simultaneously to each party. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Experts] 12.10 The cost of a report from an expert will usually be paid by the instructing party: the costs of the expert replying to questions will usually be borne by the party which asks the questions. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Experts] 12.11 If necessary, after proceedings have commenced and with the permission of the court, the parties may obtain further expert reports. It would be for the court to decide whether the costs of more than one expert’s report should be recoverable. ## Negotiations following an admission

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Negotiations following an admission] 13.1 Where a defendant admits liability which has caused some damage, before proceedings are issued, the claimant should send to that defendant—

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Negotiations following an admission] (1)any medical reports obtained under this Protocol on which the claimant relies; and

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Negotiations following an admission] (2)a schedule of any past and future expenses and losses which are claimed, even if the schedule is necessarily provisional. The schedule should contain as much detail as reasonably practicable and should identify those losses that are ongoing. If the schedule is likely to be updated before the case is concluded, it should say so. The claimant should keep the defendant informed as to the rate at which their financial loss is progressing throughout the entire Protocol period. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Negotiations following an admission] 13.2 The claimant should delay issuing proceedings for 21 days from disclosure of (1) and (2) above (unless such delay would cause his claim to become time-barred), to enable the parties to consider whether the claim is capable of settlement. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Negotiations following an admission] 13.3 CPR Part 36 permits claimants and defendants to make offers to settle pre-proceedings. Parties should always consider if it is appropriate to make a Part 36 Offer before issuing. If such an offer is made, the party making the offer must always try to supply sufficient evidence and/or information to enable the offer to be properly considered. The level of detail will depend on the value of the claim

## Alternative Dispute Resolution

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Alternative Dispute Resolution] 14.1 Litigation should be a last resort. As part of this Protocol, the parties should consider whether negotiation or some other form of Alternative Dispute Resolution (“ADR”) might enable them to resolve their dispute without commencing proceedings. ## 14.2 Some of the options for resolving disputes without commencing proceedings are—

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 14.2 Some of the options for resolving disputes without commencing proceedings are—] (1)discussions and negotiation (which may or may not include making Part 36 Offers);

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 14.2 Some of the options for resolving disputes without commencing proceedings are—] (2)mediation or conciliation – a third party facilitating a resolution; and

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 14.2 Some of the options for resolving disputes without commencing proceedings are—] (3)early neutral evaluation – a third party giving an informed opinion on the dispute. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 14.2 Some of the options for resolving disputes without commencing proceedings are—] 14.3 If proceedings are issued, the parties may be required by the court to provide evidence that ADR has been considered. It is expressly recognised that no party can or should be forced to mediate or enter into any form of ADR but unreasonable refusal to consider ADR will be taken into account by the court when deciding who bears the costs of the proceedings. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 14.2 Some of the options for resolving disputes without commencing proceedings are—] 14.4 Information on mediation and other forms of ADR is available in the Jackson ADR Handbook 2nd Edition (available from Oxford University Press) or at—

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > 14.2 Some of the options for resolving disputes without commencing proceedings are—] https://www.gov.uk/guidance/a-guide-to-civil-mediation (opens in a new tab)

## Stocktake

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Stocktake] 15.1Where the procedure set out in this Protocol has not resolved the dispute between the parties, each party should undertake a review of its own position and the strengths and weaknesses of its case. The parties should then together consider the evidence and the arguments in order to see whether litigation can be avoided or, if that is not possible, for the issues between the parties to be narrowed before proceedings are issued. The claimant or their solicitor is recommended to invite the defendant to nominate solicitors to act in the proceedings and do so by no later than 14 days before the intended issue date. ## Annex A: Template letters of claim and response

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Annex A: Template letters of claim and response] A1: Letter of claim To [Defendant] Dear Sirs, Defendants Booking Reference: Claimant’s full name and address: Dates of Holiday: Name of Hotel/Resort and board basis: Room number:

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Annex A: Template letters of claim and response] We are instructed by our above-named client to claim damages arising from gastric illness contracted whilst on holiday at the above-named hotel/resort. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Annex A: Template letters of claim and response] The circumstances giving rise to the claim are as follows;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Annex A: Template letters of claim and response] [Clear summary of the facts to include:

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Annex A: Template letters of claim and response] the nature of the illness, the symptoms, the date of onset and duration of the symptoms;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Annex A: Template letters of claim and response] whether the enjoyment of any amenities (e.g. leisure pools, dining, entertainment, tours/excursions) were denied or compromised;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Annex A: Template letters of claim and response] whether any other person who travelled on the same booking reference also fell ill.]

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Annex A: Template letters of claim and response] The reasons why we are alleging fault are:

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Annex A: Template letters of claim and response] [set out the alleged cause of the illness and why the defendant is alleged to be liable.]

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Annex A: Template letters of claim and response] [Further information to include:

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Annex A: Template letters of claim and response] Whether the claimant went on any excursions and ate or drank outside of the resort, with details if applicable. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Annex A: Template letters of claim and response] Whether any medication was taken, if so what type and over what period;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Annex A: Template letters of claim and response] Whether the illness was reported to anyone at the resort and if so to who and on what date;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Annex A: Template letters of claim and response] Whether the claimant went to their GP on returning to the UK and, if so, on what date;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Annex A: Template letters of claim and response] Whether the claimant went to the hospital on returning to the UK and, if so, on what date;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Annex A: Template letters of claim and response] If medical attention was sought were any tests carried out and was any pathogen identified. If medical treatment involved isolation, provide duration;

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Annex A: Template letters of claim and response] Whether any other guests, in the claimants booking or otherwise, reported illness and, if so, their details. ## Loss of earnings

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Loss of earnings] Our client is employed as (occupation) and has had the following time off work (dates of absence). His/Her approximate weekly income is £. ## Other financial losses

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Other financial losses] We are also aware of the following (likely) financial losses. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Other financial losses] At this stage of our enquiries we would expect the documents contained in parts (insert appropriate parts of standard disclosure list) to be relevant to this action. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Other financial losses] We expect an acknowledgement of this letter within 42 days. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Other financial losses] Yours faithfully. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Other financial losses] A2: Letter of response

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Other financial losses] To Claimant’s legal representative/ litigant in person Dear Sirs [Claimant’s name] v [Defendant’s name]

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Other financial losses] We have been instructed to act on behalf of [defendant] in relation to your client’s alleged illness whilst on holiday under booking reference [XXX]. [We note that you have also written to [defendant] in connection with this claim. We [do/do not] believe they are a relevant party because [set out reasons]. [In addition, we believe your claim should be directed against [defendant] for the following reasons:]

## Liability

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Liability] In respect of our client’s liability for this illness we admit the illness occurred and that our client is liable for loss and damage to the claimant the extent of which will require quantification. [Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Liability] Or

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Liability] admit the illness occurred but deny that our client is responsible for any loss or damage alleged to have been caused for the following reasons:

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Liability] Or

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Liability] do not admit that illness occurred either in the manner described in your letter of claim [or at all] because:

## Limitation

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Limitation] We [do not] intend to raise [a] any limitation defence. ## Documents

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Documents] We attach copies of the following documents in support of our client’s position:

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Documents] You have requested copies of the following documents which we are not enclosing as we do not believe they are relevant for the following reasons:

[Pre-action protocol for resolution of package travel claims > Documents] It would assist our investigations if you could supply us with copies of the following documents:

